1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,910
My instinct is to view the connection itself as the finish line.

2
00:00:04,430 --> 00:00:09,890
Like, if you've successfully linked the APIs, and data is actively flowing across your program,

3
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:13,550
isn't the problem technically solved? Well, no.

4
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:13,770
Mm-hmm.

5
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:19,810
Because, um, being functional under ideal conditions is not the same as being dependable under stress.

6
00:00:20,030 --> 00:00:21,250
Right. That makes sense.

7
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:29,070
In a governance framework, just stating that a digital service provider meets service levels is, uh, essentially just a wish.

8
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:30,170
A wish without teeth.

9
00:00:30,550 --> 00:00:36,330
Exactly. Without an SLA, the operating authority, the body governing the infrastructure, has no teeth at all.

10
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:41,030
The SLA is the mechanism that turns that wish into an enforceable commitment.

11
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:46,970
Which prevents your systems from becoming, you know, fragmented or siloed when things inevitably go wrong.

12
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:48,350
Yes, exactly.

13
00:00:48,970 --> 00:00:54,590
So if a general commitment is just a wish, what are the actual levers the operating authority can pull?

14
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:58,210
I mean, what are the tangible metrics you can actually plan around?

15
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:01,510
Well, it boils down to five distinct operational levers.

16
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:04,850
Number one is availability, and number two is response time.

17
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:08,970
Okay, so availability, that's just, um, whether the server's on, right?

18
00:01:09,410 --> 00:01:12,810
Not exactly. It's a strict committed uptime percentage.

19
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:13,650
Oh, wow.

20
00:01:14,370 --> 00:01:17,030
So if I demand ninety-nine point nine percent availability,

21
00:01:17,730 --> 00:01:20,549
you have to completely change your engineering to hit that.

22
00:01:21,210 --> 00:01:26,570
You hit the nail on the head. The target dictates the engineering, and that ties directly into response time.

23
00:01:26,950 --> 00:01:32,150
Because if a system is technically up, but it takes two full minutes to return a data query...

24
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:36,270
It will stall your minister's objectives just as effectively as a total outage.

25
00:01:36,470 --> 00:01:37,130
Right. Yeah.

26
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:40,470
Okay, so that covers the performance side.

27
00:01:40,770 --> 00:01:45,390
But what about the operational side? When the system does fail, how do these metrics protect you?

28
00:01:46,090 --> 00:01:47,850
That's where the final three levers come in.

29
00:01:48,190 --> 00:01:51,210
Number three, support hours. Number four, incident response.

30
00:01:51,650 --> 00:01:53,190
And number five, change notice.

31
00:01:54,170 --> 00:01:55,210
Okay. Support hours.

32
00:01:55,830 --> 00:01:59,230
That dictates exactly when a human is at the desk to help.

33
00:01:59,530 --> 00:01:59,850
Yes.

34
00:02:00,530 --> 00:02:02,790
And incident response is your escalation protocol.

35
00:02:02,850 --> 00:02:07,670
Like, who do you call upon a failure, and what is their mandatory acknowledgment speed?

36
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:08,290
Got it.

37
00:02:09,050 --> 00:02:12,230
And change notice feels like the sleeper hit of the five.

38
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:16,250
I assume that's the warning time before a provider pushes a software update?

39
00:02:16,570 --> 00:02:19,730
Exactly. So you aren't caught off guard and left with a broken dependency.

40
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:20,190
Right.

41
00:02:20,810 --> 00:02:26,090
So those five numbers working together transform a mere technical connection into a true dependency

42
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:28,630
you can actually build a citizen service on.

43
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:29,210
They do.

44
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:32,710
But, I mean, let's look at the psychology of this for a second.

45
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:39,510
If we just hand these rigid targets to a provider saying they must resolve critical incidents in eight hours,

46
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:43,750
wouldn't they just quietly ignore targets they know they can't realistically hit?

47
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:50,550
Oh, absolutely. Setting numbers for a provider rather than with them practically guarantees failure.

48
00:02:51,170 --> 00:02:52,910
Right, which creates a false sense...

49
00:02:53,290 --> 00:02:54,930
A false sense of security. Exactly.

50
00:02:55,550 --> 00:03:00,270
You're operating under the assumption you have a safety net, but that net isn't tied to anything.

51
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:05,430
An SLA that everyone quietly ignores is actually far worse than having no SLA at all.

52
00:03:06,290 --> 00:03:07,790
So you have to co-create the numbers.

53
00:03:08,490 --> 00:03:11,850
But aren't you just letting the provider set the bar artificially low then?

54
00:03:12,510 --> 00:03:20,170
There is a tension there, for sure. But you establish realistic baselines together and then raise those expectations as the platform matures.

55
00:03:20,810 --> 00:03:25,530
The policy briefing highlights a specific demonstration of a learner registry as the ideal standard here.

56
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:32,490
To give some context, a learner registry would be a centralized database for student educational records, so

57
00:03:33,210 --> 00:03:33,970
very high stakes,

58
00:03:34,570 --> 00:03:35,530
heavy daily traffic.

59
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:36,410
Exactly.

60
00:03:37,110 --> 00:03:39,650
And in their signed SLA, they didn't just guess at numbers.

61
00:03:39,930 --> 00:03:44,170
They mutually agreed to, uh, ninety-nine point five percent monthly availability.

62
00:03:44,330 --> 00:03:54,330
Wow. Okay. Yeah. Strict weekday support hours, a one-hour incident acknowledgment time, resolution within eight hours, and a mandatory five business days notice before any plan

63
00:03:54,390 --> 00:03:54,770
changes.

64
00:03:55,510 --> 00:04:02,410
So those five mutually agreed upon levers are the exact difference between a platform that merely works in a controlled demo

65
00:04:03,090 --> 00:04:04,830
and one you can actually trust.

66
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:12,510
They are. It takes high level governance obligations, forces them into engineered realities, and captures them in a template you can reuse.

